WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.170 --> 00:00:04.460
For any fisherman, it's the moment of uncertainty.

00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:08.000
Boat owner Musa Foon keeps a watchful eye
on the net as it comes in.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:16.759
Today, to his joy, the River Gambia has provided.

00:00:16.759 --> 00:00:23.600
"I really like this job, because I am jobless
before this brigade come.

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:24.970
So now I am fully engaged.

00:00:24.970 --> 00:00:26.850
I am a fulltime fisherman now.

00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:27.891
I am very happy doing it.

00:00:27.891 --> 00:00:30.670
And I really like it."

00:00:30.670 --> 00:00:35.670
Musa Foon got the money to buy his fishing
boat from the IOM, the International Organization

00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:41.420
for Migration, which has been trying to boost
the local economy in eastern Gambia.

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:45.389
It's given the 26-year-old his first steady
job since leaving school.

00:00:45.389 --> 00:00:50.229
The European Union is also pumping money into
the region, with its 11-million-euro Gambia

00:00:50.229 --> 00:00:53.359
Youth Empowerment Scheme.

00:00:53.359 --> 00:00:56.489
Musa Foon says are definite signs of improvement.

00:00:56.489 --> 00:01:02.659
With a new government in power and more development
aid, he hopes his home region will benefit.

00:01:02.659 --> 00:01:07.810
"With this system, I believe things are changing.

00:01:07.810 --> 00:01:11.950
The EU just pumping money for the youths to
do something with their lives.

00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:15.189
That's a good start."

00:01:15.189 --> 00:01:19.439
The development programs of the IOM and EU
are part of a broader strategy -- to stem

00:01:19.439 --> 00:01:22.920
the flow of migrants trying to reach Europe.

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:27.640
Last year alone more than 12-thousand Gambians
crossed the Mediterranean.

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:30.899
That from a country of less than two million
people.

00:01:30.899 --> 00:01:33.719
Stopping that trend is big task.

00:01:33.719 --> 00:01:39.380
The IOM only has a few projects up and running,
and a staff of just three.

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:42.200
"We are expanding.

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:43.200
You're right.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:45.299
Right now we're quite small.

00:01:45.299 --> 00:01:51.929
But the strategy in terms of decreasing the
numbers of migrants crossing the Mediterranean

00:01:51.929 --> 00:01:59.770
to Europe is creating opportunities in their
own countries, and of course to get the message

00:01:59.770 --> 00:02:09.780
out that it's really not worth the risk of
crossing the desert."

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:16.180
Musa Foon and his crew are returning after
a long day on the river.

00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:18.420
They have little to show for it.

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:25.379
Today's catch will earn each of them less
than a euro.

00:02:25.379 --> 00:02:30.710
That's not much financial security for a young
man supporting his parents.

00:02:30.710 --> 00:02:38.880
"Sometimes I do feel stress.

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:47.010
I feel stress thinking I really need to help
my family.

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:50.480
But I cannot.

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:52.540
The financial support is now there.

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:54.860
So I feel the stress in me sometimes."

00:02:54.860 --> 00:03:02.799
Two of Musa's siblings migrated to Europe,
and regularly send money home.

00:03:02.799 --> 00:03:07.140
Musa himself set out twice on the journey,
but failed both times.

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:10.340
His father says the family couldn't survive
without money from abroad.

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:12.939
"We cannot avoid it.

00:03:12.939 --> 00:03:14.890
Because of necessity.

00:03:14.890 --> 00:03:17.489
We have a problem.

00:03:17.489 --> 00:03:18.489
Necessity.

00:03:18.489 --> 00:03:23.349
"Why do you think it is a necessity?"

00:03:23.349 --> 00:03:29.200
"When they are here, they cannot keep them
up.

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:31.860
With the family.

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:42.599
So one has to quit up and find his way out,
to earn more, to support the rest."

00:03:42.599 --> 00:03:47.200
The next morning Musa Foon's boat is back
out on the water.

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:52.680
He says he needs to earn 100 euros a month
to make a reasonable living.

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:54.969
That's still a long way away.

00:03:54.969 --> 00:04:01.739
So like other Gambians he faces a difficult
choice -- to stick out the hardships at home,

00:04:01.739 --> 00:04:04.400
or set out, once more, for the promise of
Europe.

